doc.para:
Array Class
　The Array class is a class that handles arrays.

　You can create an object of this class and treat it like an array using subscripts specified by the indirect member selection operator. Subscripts are integers. 0 represents the first element, and 1 represents the next element. If a negative integer is specified, it is treated as specifying from the end of the array (-1 represents the last element).

	var a = new Array();
	var i;
	for(i=0;i<100;i++) a[i]=i;


　You do not need to declare the size of the array. The size is automatically expanded up to the size of the specified subscript, and elements to which no value has been assigned are initialized to void.

　In addition, there is a count property, which represents the size of the array. Assigning a value to the count property can also change the size of the array.

In-expression Arrays Using [ ], you can describe an object of the Array class on the spot within an expression. Inside [ ], list expressions that will become initial elements, separated by commas. For example,
var ar = ["a", "b", "c"];
If you write like this, a reference to an object of the Array class containing elements "a" "b" "c" will be assigned to ar.

sort
　The sort method sorts (reorders) the array.

Syntax : sort(<sort_order='+'>, <stable_sort=false>)

　The sort order is one of the following strings. In ascending order, elements are reordered from smallest to largest, and descending order is the reverse. If omitted, it is assumed that '+' is specified.

"+"  : Ascending order (Comparison with normal < operator)
"-"  : Descending order
"0"  : Numeric ascending order (10, 2, 1 are reordered to 1, 2, 10)
"9"  : Numeric descending order
"a"  : String ascending order ("a", "c", "b" are reordered to "a", "b", "c")
"z"  : String descending order

　Instead of a sort order character, a function can be specified. The function takes 2 arguments. Specify a function that returns true when the 1st argument should come before the 2nd argument (Note that the meaning of the return value is different from the function passed to JavaScript's sort method).

　In the 2nd argument, specify whether to perform a stable sort. If omitted, false is assumed to be specified, and an unstable sort is performed. In a stable sort, array elements with the same rank retain their relative order after sorting. In an unstable sort, there is no guarantee that array elements with the same rank retain their relative order after sorting.

	var array = [ 0, 1, 2, 10, 12, 14, 4, 6, 2 ];
	array.sort();
		// ↑ After method execution, array becomes [0, 1, 2, 2, 4, 6, 10, 12, 14]
	array.sort('-');
		// ↑ After method execution, array becomes [14, 12, 10, 6, 4, 2, 2, 1, 0]
	array.sort('a');
		// ↑ After method execution, array becomes [0, 1, 10, 12, 14, 2, 2, 4, 6]

	var array = [ "hoge", "foo" , "bar", "baz" ];
	array.sort();
		// ↑ After method execution, array becomes ["bar", "baz", "foo", "hoge"]

	var array = [ "abcd", "abc", "ab", "a" , "b", "c" ];
	array.sort(function(a,b) { return a< b; } );
		// ↑ Same meaning as specifying '+'

	var array = [ "abcd", "abc", "ab", "a" , "b", "c" ];
	array.sort(function(a,b) { return a.length < b.length; } );
		// ↑ In order of shortest string length
		// (There is no guarantee that "a" "b" "c" of the same length
		// will be in "a" "b" "c" order after sorting)

	var array = [ "abcd", "abc", "ab", "a" , "b", "c" ];
	array.sort(function(a,b) { return a.length < b.length; }, true );
		// ↑ In order of shortest string length
		// ("a" "b" "c" of the same length will remain in this order after sorting)
